# The SIGIL toolchain sigil-pi is built and tested against.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# sigil-pi is not self-contained: every tool is forged by a sigil-mcp/sigil-serve
# binary built from a sibling SIGIL checkout ($SIGIL_ROOT, default ../SIGIL).
# Nothing used to record WHICH toolchain. On 2026-07-31 a rebuild of that sibling
# turned the whole suite red (44 failures) with no change to sigil-pi at all, and
# there was no way to tell what had moved. Recording it — and checking it in
# ci.sh — turns a baffling failure into a one-line diagnosis.
#
# WHY TREE HASHES, NOT A COMMIT SHA
# SIGIL commits to bench/, docs/ and rl_tasks/ constantly; none of that can change
# the forge binary. A commit pin trips on every one of them (it did, within three
# minutes of being written) and would train everyone to ignore it. These are the
# git tree hashes of the only two directories the binary is built from, so the
# check fires if and only if the toolchain itself actually moved.
#   git -C $SIGIL_ROOT rev-parse HEAD:crates HEAD:stdlib
#
# FORMAT: one `key = value` per line. Blank lines and `#` comments ignored.

crates = bf4570aecf26ec66fa421cf964dcc481db1f0df4
stdlib = 82c9098968dcbd32976f3d97af189dc7b1f9f483

# THE SECOND PIN MODE: sha256_<platform>
#
# The tree hashes above can only be checked by someone who can CLONE SIGIL.
# That was fine while a checkout was the only way to have a toolchain, and it
# is the reason sigil-pi could not be installed by anyone outside the private
# repo (issue #15). An installed toolchain has no tree to hash, so it is
# pinned by the digest of the binary instead:
#
#   sha256_linux_x86_64 = <64 hex chars>
#   sha256_macos_arm64  = <64 hex chars>
#
# Platform keys are `<system>_<machine>` as toolchain.platform_key() computes
# them (x86_64/amd64 and arm64/aarch64 normalised to one spelling each), one
# line per published platform — a single digest cannot describe two builds,
# and a pin that silently matched the wrong platform would be worse than none.
#
# WHICH MODE APPLIES is decided by what actually resolved, never assumed:
# ci.sh checks the tree hashes because it rebuilds from source, and `main()`
# checks the sha256 because it runs whatever was installed. An ABSENT key is
# not a failure — there are no published binaries yet, and treating "nothing
# to check" as tampering would make the first release unrunnable. It does mean
# an installed toolchain is currently UNVERIFIED, which is the honest state
# and changes the day SIGIL publishes.

# Human-readable note shown when the check fails.
note = claude/agentic-bench; @Flow taint polymorphism in json:: + wasmtime 47.0.3

# A commit that CONTAINS the trees above. Convenience only — CI checks this out
# as a candidate and the tree hashes above remain the authority. Local commits
# are fine for local use, but CI can only fetch what is PUSHED.
#
# MUST BE THE FULL 40-CHAR SHA. actions/checkout only treats a value as a
# commit at full length; anything shorter it reads as a branch/tag name,
# fetches refs/heads/<ref>*, matches nothing, and fails with "The process
# '/usr/bin/git' failed with exit code 1" — naming neither the ref nor the
# cause. An abbreviated ref sat here through five PRs and was invisible until
# the forge job first actually ran (2026-08-02). Guarded now, both in
# tests/test_guards.py and the standalone CI job.
#
# PUSHED: eb9f1715 is the tip of origin/claude/agentic-bench, so the
# pinned crates/ and stdlib/ trees are now fetchable from CI. Steps 1 and 3 of
# the .github/workflows/ci.yml header are satisfied.
#
# THE `forge` JOB NOW RUNS. It also needs the `SIGIL_REPO_TOKEN` secret —
# SIGIL is private — and without it the job is SKIPPED at the job level (it
# used to report a GREEN check in ~7s while running nothing, measured on
# PR #7; the skip is visible in the PR checks). That secret is configured:
# the job first ran on 2026-08-02 — which is how the abbreviated `ref` above
# was caught — and has run the full `./ci.sh` on every push to main since,
# ~3 minutes green (run #26, f990d1d, 2026-08-06). A local `./ci.sh` is no
# longer the only real verification.
#
# The paragraph this replaces claimed the opposite while the one above it
# already dated the first forge run. If the secret is ever removed, the job
# reverts to a visible SKIP rather than a false green — check the PR checks,
# not this comment, for what actually ran.
ref = eb9f1715dd3fadd3c9fe1f6ccb19c17f49a2ac0b

# RESOLVED AT THIS TOOLCHAIN (was the standing known issue here):
#   The `json` stdlib declared its parameters with no taint annotation, so they
#   defaulted to @Public and the tightened checker (f7fefd8f, 2026-07-21) rejected
#   the @Internal HTTP-response data parse_reply.sigil and chat_turn.sigil feed
#   them — 44 failures with no change to sigil-pi at all.
#
#   Fixed upstream in 801d0e1d, the way the note here asked for: `json::` is now
#   taint-POLYMORPHIC via a new `@Flow` annotation — the output label follows the
#   input label, so parsing @Internal bytes yields @Internal bytes and parsing
#   @Secret bytes yields @Secret bytes. Soundness is per-instantiation: each body
#   is checked once per admissible label, so a leak fails at the @Internal
#   instantiation. `kv::` and `http::`, whose returns were already @Internal, got
#   the matching @Internal on their parameters.
#
#   No declassify() was added anywhere in sigil-pi. parse_reply remains the
#   zero-grant inner-ring component with no downgrade in its data path — the
#   constraint the previous note set, met rather than traded away.
#
#   Pinning BACK to a pre-2026-07-21 toolchain is therefore no longer a live
#   option to consider: it is now strictly a downgrade, giving up both the
#   taint-soundness fixes that closed fail-open T001 holes (16f93fd2, bc1cfe25,
#   1eb5d516) and this fix, for nothing.
#
# NOTE: tools/chat_turn.sigil INLINES stdlib http + kv at generation time
# (make_chat_turn.py -> compose_with_stdlib). Moving the pinned stdlib/ tree
# therefore requires regenerating it, or ci.sh step 1 fails as stale.
